WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.289
Authentication answers the question, who
is making this request.

2
00:00:07.369 --> 00:00:14.521
In a Razor Pages application,
authentication may be based on cookies,

3
00:00:14.601 --> 00:00:19.814
an identity provider, an enterprise
sign-on flow,

4
00:00:19.894 --> 00:00:24.864
or another ASP.NET Core authentication
scheme.

5
00:00:24.944 --> 00:00:33.187
Whatever the mechanism, identity should
come from the configured platform path

6
00:00:33.267 --> 00:00:41.632
rather than from page naming, hidden
fields, route values, or local assumptions.

7
00:00:41.712 --> 00:00:46.439
Authorization answers a different
question:

8
00:00:46.519 --> 00:00:51.126
what is this identity allowed to access or
do.

9
00:00:51.206 --> 00:00:57.631
A signed-in user may be allowed to view
one page but not another.

10
00:00:57.711 --> 00:01:06.075
A support user may view selected records
but not approve administrative changes.

11
00:01:06.155 --> 00:01:14.641
An administrator may manage configuration
but still need extra checks for certain

12
00:01:14.721 --> 00:01:15.933
workflows.

13
00:01:16.013 --> 00:01:22.681
Treating login as blanket permission is a
common design mistake.

14
00:01:22.761 --> 00:01:30.762
Razor Pages can apply authorization by
page, folder, convention, and policy.

15
00:01:30.842 --> 00:01:39.691
Folder conventions are especially useful
when the directory structure reflects real

16
00:01:39.771 --> 00:01:41.832
access boundaries.

17
00:01:41.912 --> 00:01:48.943
Public pages can remain public, account
pages can require identity,

18
00:01:49.023 --> 00:01:54.235
administrative areas can require named
policies,

19
00:01:54.315 --> 00:02:02.559
and sensitive workflows can use explicit
requirements that reviewers can find.

20
00:02:02.639 --> 00:02:09.549
Anonymous access should be deliberate,
narrow, and easy to review.

21
00:02:09.629 --> 00:02:15.447
Pages such as sign-in, password recovery,
help content,

22
00:02:15.527 --> 00:02:20.861
or public documentation may need anonymous
access.

23
00:02:20.941 --> 00:02:24.457
The important point is ownership.

24
00:02:24.537 --> 00:02:33.265
Anonymous pages should not appear
accidentally because a convention was omitted or a

25
00:02:33.345 --> 00:02:36.982
page was placed in the wrong folder.

26
00:02:37.062 --> 00:02:45.426
Policy-based authorization helps when
access depends on more than a simple role.

27
00:02:45.506 --> 00:02:52.659
Policies can represent claims, tenant
context, approval authority,

28
00:02:52.739 --> 00:03:01.345
employment status, support scope,
environment, or custom business requirements.

29
00:03:01.425 --> 00:03:09.669
A meaningful policy name also helps
reviewers understand why access is allowed.

30
00:03:09.749 --> 00:03:17.265
User interface restrictions do not enforce
server-side authorization.

31
00:03:17.345 --> 00:03:23.285
Hiding a link or disabling a button can
improve usability,

32
00:03:23.365 --> 00:03:29.547
but the page handler must still enforce
access on the server.

33
00:03:29.627 --> 00:03:37.992
Sensitive workflows and administrative
actions should be safe even when someone

34
00:03:38.072 --> 00:03:44.618
reaches the handler directly through
normal browser behavior.

35
00:03:44.698 --> 00:03:50.032
Some decisions require resource-specific
checks.

36
00:03:50.112 --> 00:03:53.991
A user may be allowed to view invoices,

37
00:03:54.071 --> 00:03:59.405
but only invoices they own or are assigned
to manage.

38
00:03:59.485 --> 00:04:06.395
A page handler that loads a record should
verify ownership, tenant,

39
00:04:06.475 --> 00:04:15.203
workflow state, or permission against that
specific record before returning data or

40
00:04:15.283 --> 00:04:16.980
changing state.
